{:edb/db-before-t 1275 :edb/db-after-t 1276 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534588 50 #inst "2026-10-08T01:47:09.062Z" 13194139534588 true] [17592186096464 1100 "Find $\\mathbf F(t)$ given that $\\mathbf F'(t)= \\langle 6t^5+2t, \\sec^2 t\\rangle$." 13194139534588 false] [17592186096464 1100 "Find $\\mathbf F(t)$ given that $\\mathbf F'(t)= \\langle 6t^5+2t, \\sec^2 t\\rangle.$" 13194139534588 true] [17592186096464 1145 "Note that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle 6t^5+2t,\\, \\sec^2 t\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫⟨6t^{5} + 2t, \\sec^{2} t⟩dt &= ⟨∫(6t^{5} + 2t)dt, ∫\\sec^{2} tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{6} + t^{2} + c_{1}, \\tan t + c_{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{6} + t^{2}, \\tan t⟩ + ⟨c_{1}, c_{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{6} + t^{2}, \\tan t⟩ + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139534588 false] [17592186096464 1145 "Note that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle 6t^5+2t,\\, \\sec^2 t\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨6{t}^{5}+2t,\\,{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int (6{t}^{5}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int {sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2}+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139534588 true] [17592186096465 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\left\\langle\\cos(2t+1), \\, 4\\right\\rangle \\,\\textrm d t\\,$." 13194139534588 false] [17592186096465 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\left\\langle\\cos(2t+1), \\, 4\\right\\rangle \\,\\textrm d t\\,.$" 13194139534588 true] [17592186096465 1145 "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1),\\,4⟩\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x(t) =\\cos(2t+1),$ we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u= 2t +1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d t = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)+{c}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1)+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t) =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int 4\\text{d}t=4t+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1),\\,4⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1)+{c}_{1},\\,4t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1),\\,4t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1),\\,4t⟩+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534588 true] [17592186096465 1145 "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n∫⟨\\cos (2t + 1), 4⟩dt = ⟨∫\\cos (2t + 1)dt, ∫4dt⟩\n$$\n\n- For $x(t) =\\cos(2t+1)$, we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u= 2t +1$. Then\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d t = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u$.\nTherefore, we have\n$∫x(t)dt|= ∫\\cos (2t + 1)dt; = ∫\\cos u \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du; = \\frac{1}{2}∫\\cos udu; = \\frac{1}{2}(\\sin u) + c_{1}; = \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t + 1) + c_{1}$.\n- For $y(t) =4$, we have\n$∫y(t)dt = ∫4dt = 4t + c_{2}$.\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫⟨\\cos (2t + 1), 4⟩dt &= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t + 1) + c_{1}, 4t + c_{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t + 1), 4t⟩ + ⟨c_{1}, c_{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t + 1), 4t⟩ + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534588 false] [17592186096466 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2} \\left\\langle 2e^{t},\\, 6t^2\\right\\rangle\\, \\textrm d t$." 13194139534588 false] [17592186096466 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2} \\left\\langle 2e^{t},\\, 6t^2\\right\\rangle\\, \\textrm d t.$" 13194139534588 true] [17592186096466 1145 "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}⟨2e^{t}, 6t^{2}⟩dt &= ⟨∫_{0}^{2}2e^{t}dt, ∫_{0}^{2}6t^{2}dt⟩. \\\\\n&= ⟨2e^{t} \\mid _{0}^{2}, 2t^{3} \\mid _{0}^{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨[2e^{2} - 2e^{0}], [2 \\cdot 2^{3} - 0]⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2e^{2} - 2, 16⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534588 false] [17592186096466 1145 "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,6{t}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩. \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{t}\\,\\,{|}_{0}^{2},\\,2{t}^{3}\\,\\,{|}_{0}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨[2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{0}],\\,[2\\cdot {2}^{3}-0]⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{2}-2,\\,16⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534588 true] [17592186096467 1100 "Calculate $\\mathbf F(t)$ given that $\\mathbf F'(t)= \\left\\langle \\sec t \\tan t, \\,  \\sin(2t)\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf F(0)=\\langle 0,\\, 0\\rangle.$" 13194139534588 true] [17592186096467 1100 "Calculate $\\mathbf F(t)$ given that $\\mathbf F'(t)= \\left\\langle \\sec t \\tan t, \\, \\sin(2t)\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf F(0)=\\langle 0,\\, 0\\rangle$." 13194139534588 false] [17592186096467 1145 "Note that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle \\sec t \\tan t,\\, \\sin(2t)\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫⟨\\sec t\\tan t, \\sin (2t)⟩dt &= ⟨∫\\sec t\\tan tdt, ∫\\sin (2t)dt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\sec t + c_{1},-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos (2t) + c_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(0)=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(0) = ⟨\\sec 0 + c_{1},-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos (2 \\cdot 0) + c_{2}⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩ \\\\\n⟨1 + c_{1},-\\frac{1}{2} + c_{2}⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $c_1=-1$ and\n\n$$\nc_2=\\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\left\\langle \\sec t - 1, \\, -\\dfrac 1 2\\cos(2t)+ \\dfrac12\\right\\rangle\n$$" 13194139534588 false] [17592186096467 1145 "Note that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle \\sec t \\tan t,\\,  \\sin(2t)\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $\\mathbf F(0)=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0 \\right\\rangle$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0)=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨0,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=\\dfrac 12.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\left\\langle  \\sec t - 1  , \\,  -\\dfrac 1 2\\cos(2t)+ \\dfrac12\\right\\rangle.\n$$" 13194139534588 true] [17592186096756 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297269 13194139534588 true] [17592186096756 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297276 13194139534588 true] [17592186096756 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297336 13194139534588 true] [17592186096757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297283 13194139534588 true] [17592186096757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297329 13194139534588 true] [17592186096757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297343 13194139534588 true] [17592186096757 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297350 13194139534588 true] [17592186096758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297290 13194139534588 true] [17592186096758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297302 13194139534588 true] [17592186096758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297308 13194139534588 true] [17592186096758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297313 13194139534588 true] [17592186096758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297357 13194139534588 true] [17592186096759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297297 13194139534588 true] [17592186096759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297318 13194139534588 true] [17592186096759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297323 13194139534588 true] [17592186096759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297364 13194139534588 true] [17592186096759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297371 13194139534588 true] [17592186097225 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534588 true] [17592186097225 1100 "If $F^{′}(t) = ⟨e^{t} - 4, \\sin t⟩$, then $F(t) =$" 13194139534588 false] [17592186097225 1100 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$, then $F(t)=$" 13194139534588 true] [17592186097225 1145 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int ({e}^{t}-4)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139534588 true] [17592186097226 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534588 true] [17592186097226 1100 "Find $\\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139534588 true] [17592186097226 1100 "Find $∫⟨e^{t}, \\cos t⟩dt$." 13194139534588 false] [17592186097226 1145 "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534588 true] [17592186097285 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534588 true] [17592186097285 1100 "If $F^{′}(t) = ⟨3t^{2}, e^{t}⟩$, then $F(t) =$" 13194139534588 false] [17592186097285 1100 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" 13194139534588 true] [17592186097285 1145 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}+{c}_{1},\\,{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3},\\,{e}^{t}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3},\\,{e}^{t}⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139534588 true] [17592186297269 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297270 13194139534588 true] [17592186297269 1089 "q-123224" 13194139534588 true] [17592186297269 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534588 true] [17592186297269 1100 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" 13194139534588 true] [17592186297269 1113 #uuid "52390093-006a-5954-b55c-8b4eeb61a32f" 13194139534588 true] [17592186297269 1145 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-v